<section class="text-card" aria-label="Text A">
  <h2>Text A</h2>
  <p>Urban flooding is usually described as a weather problem. It is better understood as a land-surface problem that weather reveals.</p>
  <p>Undeveloped ground behaves like a sponge. Rainfall infiltrates, is held in soil and shallow groundwater, and is released gradually over days. Impervious surfaces such as roads, roofs and parking areas remove almost all of that infiltration, converting a slow subsurface process into rapid surface runoff.</p>
  <p>Two further factors compound the effect. First, urban growth in Chennai has repeatedly occurred on wetlands and floodplains, which are precisely the parts of the landscape that previously absorbed and buffered floodwater. Second, drainage infrastructure is designed against historical rainfall intensities, and those intensities are changing.</p>
  <p>The result is that a storm of a given size now produces more runoff, more quickly, across a landscape with less capacity to hold it than it had several decades ago. Understanding urban flooding therefore requires attention to what has been built, and on what, rather than to rainfall totals alone.</p>
</section>

<section class="text-card" aria-label="Text B">
  <h2>Text B</h2>
  <p>A city does not flood only because it rains.</p>
  <p>When rain lands on soil, most of it soaks in. Some evaporates. Whatever is left runs across the surface. Pave that soil and the balance breaks. Asphalt absorbs almost nothing, so water that used to go down goes sideways instead, fast, toward whatever is lowest.</p>
  <p>Cities tend to build on what is lowest. Flat ground beside a marsh is cheap and easy to develop, which is why it gets developed. Pallikaranai marsh covered about 5,500 hectares in the 1960s. It is under 700 now. A marsh that size held an enormous volume of water through a monsoon and let it go slowly. That capacity did not move somewhere else when the marsh was built on. It stopped existing.</p>
  <p>Storm drains are meant to make up the difference. They are sized against the rainfall records available when they were designed, which means they are sized against a past that is no longer a good guide.</p>
  <p>Rain is the trigger. The surface, and what used to be under it, decide what the rain does.</p>
  <p class="text-card-note">AI use: this text was drafted with an AI language model, then checked and edited by the author.</p>
</section>

